    "created_at": "2022-09-11T16:03:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-09-11T22:48:23Z",
    "body": "I’ve needed to compose timeout-related signals and other signals many times. In some cases this did mean just one timeout + its creator had controller access, but very often the relationships were more complex than that (or became more complex than that later) — e.g. branching where a signal from above (controller not passed down) could end up being one of multiple “abort factors” for its “descendants”.\r\n\r\nWe eventually ended up with a set of compositional utils for merging signals (and sometimes, but less frequently, controllers). These often had clear symmetry with static Promise methods. With a small library of those utils, managing AC/AS eventually became a breeze ... but it took time to land on the right mental model & understand what was really called for. This learning curve was reminiscent to me of the period years ago when lots of folks were fuzzy on how to use Promise effectively & tended to flip the resolvers/promise relationship around. If the Promise constructor had introduced a timeout argument then, I don’t think it would have helped the situation. In that analogy, controller.abort is more or less `reject` — it belongs to (or is) the “executor” and if I want to model an eventual time and an eventual [something else], I likely want two Promises or two AbortSignals.\r\n\r\n(The absence of `any`, `race`, and so on though — that’s the stuff that really worked for us & kept the code clear over time — may be keeping this symmetry kinda hidden imo.)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
